<Base
  title="Who Wrote What"
  description="Most of the writing on this site is drafted by an AI agent from my own records, and I review all of it before it's published. How to tell which words are mine."
  track="plain"
  counterpart="/technical/how-this-site-is-built/#authorship"
>
  <h1>Who Wrote What</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Most of the prose on this site is drafted by an AI coding agent, working from records I keep of my own work.
    I read all of it and decide what gets published. This page says what that does and doesn't mean, and how to
    find the words that are mine alone.
  </p>

  <h2>What The Agent Writes</h2>
  <p>
    The stories, the case studies, the timelines, and the text on pages like this one start as an agent's
    draft. The agent works from my private session records: what I set out to do, what happened, and what I said
    about it. It also follows a style guide built from my own writing, so a draft is meant to sound like me.
    It's still an agent's draft.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every page written that way says so at the bottom: "Written with an AI agent; every claim reviewed by me."
  </p>

  <h2>What "Reviewed" Means</h2>
  <p>
    The first wording offered to me said "checked". I changed it to "reviewed", because checked would have
    claimed more than I do.
  </p>
  <blockquote class="own-words">
    <p class="own-words-label">In my own words</p>
    <p>
      As I'm not actually doing any kind of comprehensive check on a claim, like a fact or number, by myself, so
      all I can really claim is that I am reviewing everything presented in prose on this site, and deciding to
      publish it; but I am not necessarily asserting that I approve of the claim or that the claim is factually
      accurate.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>
    So a claim on this site rests on the records it was drafted from, and on my having read it before it went
    out. It doesn't rest on my re-checking each fact or number by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>How To Tell My Own Words</h2>
  <p>There are two ways.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A passage quoted from me is set apart and labeled "In my own words", like the one above. It's what I
      wrote, as I wrote it. If something is left out of a passage, the gap is marked.
    </li>
    <li>
      A page I wrote entirely myself says so at the bottom, in place of the line about the agent. There aren't
      any of those yet.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    On a page that has words of mine, a button at the top, "My words", brings those passages forward and sets
    the rest of the page back. Press it again to put the page back as it was.
  </p>
  <p>A part of the site for my own writing only is planned.</p>

  <h2>Why Say All This</h2>
  <p>When the agent was writing the first version of this site, I wrote this in my notes:</p>
  <blockquote class="own-words">
    <p class="own-words-label">In my own words</p>
    <p>
      I'm not sure how to feel, or how my potential readers might feel with the portfolio site content being ai
      generated. I will want to guard against ai slop on the portfolio site!
    </p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>A month later, deciding between a short label on every page and a page like this one, I wrote:</p>
  <blockquote class="own-words">
    <p class="own-words-label">In my own words</p>
    <p>
      This way, I'm bounding the "ai slop" argument within the framing of why I actually value the agent writing
      some of the content now, which is the more honest disclosure than just saying an agent wrote it, right?
    </p>
  </blockquote>
